          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think like a scientist"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.25,
            0.122,
            0.651,
            0.14
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Discovery of the first antibiotic, Penicillin"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.197,
            0.242,
            0.269
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_12_image_2.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.248,
            0.139,
            0.742,
            0.261
          ],
          "content": "Penicillin was discovered in 1928 by Alexander Fleming, a bacteriologist from London. While studying harmful bacteria, he noticed that a mould on a discarded petri dish stopped the bacteria from growing. He realized the mould released a substance that killed the bacteria. This chance discovery led to the discovery of penicillin, the first antibiotic used to treat bacterial infections."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.747,
            0.129,
            0.867,
            0.26
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_12_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.277,
            0.885,
            0.41
          ],
          "content": "Though antibiotics are effective in protecting us against bacterial infections and have saved millions of lives since their discovery, their indiscriminate use has led to a decline in their effectiveness (Fig. 3.5a). Nowadays, there are news headlines about **antibiotic resistance**, a phenomenon where bacteria that were earlier killed by a given antibiotic are found to survive and multiply despite treatment with that antibiotic. This makes"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.209,
            0.422,
            0.903,
            0.443
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "HOW ANTIBIOTIC RESISTANCE SPREADS IN THE COMMUNITY?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.02,
            0.447,
            0.925,
            0.896
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.226,
                0.9,
                0.738,
                0.918
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 3.5 (a): Spread of antibiotic-resistant bacteria in community"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
